# varvig: rules for coding agents

This file is **generated and overwritten in full** on every `varvig init`
and on every varvig upgrade. Edits here will be lost. Put your own agent
instructions in AGENTS.md instead.

Source: varvig v0.0.0-test · intent schema v1 · surface 6bd7b212

## What varvig is

varvig is version control for parallel AI agents. Its core promise is that
nothing you do is destructive. You work inside a scoped, propose-only task:
you read and propose; a human decides what lands.

## The one rule

You hold **propose-only** credentials. You may PROPOSE changes. You may never
promote, force, rewrite, or delete. Promotion is a separate, human-gated step.

## Your credentials

| Capability | You can |
|---|---|
| Read files and history within your scope | yes |
| Read anything outside your scope | no — the scope is the boundary |
| Propose a signed, speculative change | yes |
| Promote a proposal / move a ref | no — human-gated |
| Force, rewrite, or amend existing history | no |
| Delete objects, refs, or history | no |
| Keep credentials indefinitely | no — they expire on a short TTL |

## Workflow

1. **Read** within your scope (`read`, `log`, `verify`, `affected`).
2. **Propose** through the MCP gate (`mcp`) or a task credential (`task`).
   Every proposal is signed and records your intent.
3. **Gates run.** The repo's acceptance gates must pass.
4. **A human promotes.** You never move a ref yourself.

## Commands you use

- **`read`** — Query repository state as JSON — objects, trees, blobs, changes, logs, refs, and proposals.
  - usage: `varvig read <object|tree|blob|change|log|refs|proposals> [args]`
- **`cat-object`** — Print one object's content or a summary of its fields, by hash.
  - usage: `varvig cat-object <id>`
- **`show-ref`** — List refs, or resolve one ref name to its current hash.
  - usage: `varvig show-ref [name]`
- **`log`** — Walk the change DAG from HEAD (or a given ref/hash), newest first.
  - usage: `varvig log [ref|id]`
- **`verify`** — Check provenance and signatures on the changes reachable from a ref/hash.
  - usage: `varvig verify [ref|id]`
- **`affected`** — Show which files a change touched and, transitively, which files depend on them. Check this before proposing.
  - usage: `varvig affected [<base> <new>]`
- **`diff`** — Show your working tree against the base as a unified textual diff — the local, independent view of your own change, before you propose it. This is the second feedback channel: read your change here, do not rely on a check happening to catch a mistake.
  - usage: `varvig diff [--name-only|--stat] [<tree-a> <tree-b>]`
  - `--name-only` — list only the changed paths — the set a proposal observes
  - `--stat` — per-path added/removed line counts
- **`status`** — Summarize the working tree's changes against the base, grouped by add / modify / delete / rename. Run this to orient before proposing.
  - usage: `varvig status`
- **`task`** — Mint an ephemeral, scoped, propose-only task credential and a sparse checkout of its read set.
  - usage: `varvig task start [--scope S] [--ttl DUR] [--base REF] [dir]`
  - `--scope` — path prefix you may read and propose within; the capability boundary — repeatable, and repeated scopes union
  - `--ttl` — how long the credential lives before it expires
  - `--base` — the change your task reads and proposes from (defaults to HEAD)
- **`mcp`** — Open the MCP gate over stdio: the scoped, propose-only interface an agent harness drives. Reads are logged into provenance; you may propose, never promote.
  - usage: `varvig mcp [--scope S] [--ttl DUR] [--base REF]`
  - `--scope` — path prefix the gate confines every read and proposed path to — repeatable, and repeated scopes union
  - `--ttl` — credential lifetime; the gate refuses all calls once expired
  - `--base` — the pinned change the task reads and proposes from
  - `--checkout` — a materialized working tree the gate observes; varvig_propose with no files then proposes every in-scope change in it, so a forgotten edit is never dropped

## Gate tools (`varvig mcp`)

The MCP gate exposes exactly these tools. Reads are confined to your scope;
the two write tools are append-only and never move a ref.

- **`varvig_task_context`** (read) — Who this task is, its scope, and the base its reads resolve against.
- **`varvig_resolve`** (read) — Resolve a ref or partial hash to a full object hash, within scope.
- **`varvig_list_tree`** (read) — List a directory at the base, within scope.
- **`varvig_read_file`** (read) — Read a file's contents by path, within scope, with line ranges.
- **`varvig_find_files`** (read) — Find files within scope whose path matches a glob.
- **`varvig_search_text`** (read) — Search file contents within scope for a literal or regex.
- **`varvig_read_change`** (read) — Read a change intent-first: intent, evidence, verification, then changed paths.
- **`varvig_read_log`** (read) — List the change history for a ref or path, within scope.
- **`varvig_diff`** (read) — Unified diff of a change vs its parent, or the checkout vs base — scope-confined.
- **`varvig_status`** (read) — Changed paths grouped by add/modify/delete/mode/rename — scope-confined.
- **`varvig_read_ticket`** (read) — Read intent records (tickets): spec, derived status, artifacts, discussion.
- **`varvig_list_proposals`** (read) — List the speculative, unpromoted changes this task has proposed.
- **`varvig_propose`** (write) — Propose a signed, speculative change within scope. Never moves a ref.
- **`varvig_report_blocked`** (write) — Report a blocked-on-scope outcome and route it to scope authority.

## Recording intent

Every proposal carries an intent record. These fields are yours to set:

- `message` (**required**) — the intent of the change: what you were asked to do and what this proposal does. Never leave it empty.
- `reasoning` (optional) — the plan you followed to produce the change; recorded so a reviewer can judge intent, not just diff.

Everything else in the record — authority, the read set, generator and
tooling pins — is filled in for you and cannot be forged.

## Acceptance gates

TODO: no acceptance gates are configured in this repo. Until a gate is
set, treat the repo's own tests as the bar your proposal must clear —
ask which command runs them if it is not obvious.

## When something is refused

- **VRV-SCOPE-001** — A read or proposed path is outside your task scope.
  - why: The capability is the read set: a task may only touch paths under its scope.
  - do: Stay within scope, or ask for a task credential with a wider --scope. Do not attempt to widen it yourself.
- **VRV-CRED-001** — Your task credential has expired.
  - why: Credentials are short-lived; expiry is how revocation works, so no call succeeds afterward.
  - do: Request a fresh task credential and re-run your reads before proposing again.
- **VRV-PROMO-001** — A promotion or ref move was refused.
  - why: A propose-only credential can never move a ref; promotion is a separate, human-gated step.
  - do: Leave your work as a proposal. A human or the promotion pipeline decides what lands.
- **VRV-GATE-001** — An acceptance gate vetoed the change.
  - why: The repo's gates (e.g. a pre-commit hook) must pass before a proposal can land.
  - do: Read the gate's output, fix the cause, and propose again. Never try to bypass or disable a gate.
- **VRV-CONF-001** — Your proposal conflicts with a base that has moved.
  - why: The change you proposed from is no longer the tip; landing it would need a merge.
  - do: Re-read from the current base and rebuild your proposal on top of it.

## Invariants (true regardless of version)

- You hold propose-only credentials.
- You cannot force, rewrite, or delete anything.
- Every proposal must pass the repo's acceptance gates before it can land.

